6
00:00:20,053 --> 00:00:21,971
So first of all, what is Yocto?

7
00:00:21,971 --> 00:00:25,554
Yocto is similar to what we've
already discussed with but

8
00:00:25,554 --> 00:00:29,581
it's really a way to roll your own Linux
distribution for embedded devices.

9
00:00:29,581 --> 00:00:34,573
So as kind of implied by the name is
all about building ripped file systems.

10
00:00:34,573 --> 00:00:37,260
Yocto is a little bit more ambitious,
in this case,

11
00:00:37,260 --> 00:00:41,612
you're actually just building an entire
distribution for your embedded project.

12
00:00:41,612 --> 00:00:45,495
So as part of building your Yocto image,
you're going to actually build re

13
00:00:45,495 --> 00:00:50,028
distributable packages for every piece of
software that you include in your image.

14
00:00:50,028 --> 00:00:54,607
Very similar to if you are building
your own distribution like Debian or

15
00:00:54,607 --> 00:00:59,202
something like one of the standard
Linux distributions does already.

16
00:00:59,202 --> 00:01:01,472
It was launched in 2011, so

17
00:01:01,472 --> 00:01:06,865
it's relatively new compared to build
up which was in the early 2000s.

18
00:01:06,865 --> 00:01:11,460
And it includes embedded build tools and
a distribution called Poky.

19
00:01:11,460 --> 00:01:16,416
And I pronounce this wrong until I
started researching how to say this, but

20
00:01:16,416 --> 00:01:20,206
the right way to pronounce it
as Poky sounds like hockey.

21
00:01:20,206 --> 00:01:24,801
So that's really like
the equivalent of of Debian but or

22
00:01:24,801 --> 00:01:30,591
any Linux distribution but
it's customized for embedded projects.

23
00:01:30,591 --> 00:01:36,129
So if you just want a distribution you can
use to get started with for your embedded

24
00:01:36,129 --> 00:01:41,270
project, you can use Poky and
that's what we'll use for our assignment.

25
00:01:41,270 --> 00:01:45,896
The build system is licensed MIT,
which is a more permissive license than

26
00:01:45,896 --> 00:01:50,239
licensed used by if you
remember is licensed GPL.

27
00:01:50,239 --> 00:01:54,825
And so what this means is that there's no
expectation from the developers that you

28
00:01:54,825 --> 00:01:58,746
would share the source code that's
related to building your device or

29
00:01:58,746 --> 00:02:01,817
any of the customization you
make to the Yocto itself.

30
00:02:01,817 --> 00:02:06,690
It uses textile configurations and
the bitbake tool which is based

31
00:02:06,690 --> 00:02:11,140
on a combination of Python and
Bash in order to do the builds.

32
00:02:11,140 --> 00:02:13,519
And it builds almost
everything from source,

33
00:02:13,519 --> 00:02:15,783
including all the build tools themselves.

34
00:02:15,783 --> 00:02:21,474
Whereas build it defaults
to using binary built for

35
00:02:21,474 --> 00:02:24,259
some of the build tools.

36
00:02:24,259 --> 00:02:27,885
And part of the reason Yocto builds
everything from sources that one of

37
00:02:27,885 --> 00:02:31,024
the goals of the tool is to
support binary reproducibility.

38
00:02:31,024 --> 00:02:36,453
In other words, they want you to be able
to check in of your build system and

39
00:02:36,453 --> 00:02:41,094
have anybody be able to build from
scratch and get the exact same

40
00:02:41,094 --> 00:02:45,667
build output correspondent,
that particular commit hash.

41
00:02:45,667 --> 00:02:50,676
And so by building all the source
tools from scratch, you're less

42
00:02:50,676 --> 00:02:55,961
likely to have a slight variations
between different build systems.

43
00:02:58,940 --> 00:03:02,899
So the basic steps for setting up
