84
00:05:40,055 --> 00:05:45,462
So a little bit more about
some Yocto terminology.

85
00:05:45,462 --> 00:05:49,440
We mentioned the bitbake utility as
the utility that performs built.

86
00:05:49,440 --> 00:05:50,950
How would you bake bits?

87
00:05:50,950 --> 00:05:54,442
You make bits using recipes just
the same way that as you bake bread.

88
00:05:54,442 --> 00:05:56,275
So the Yocto terminology for

89
00:05:56,275 --> 00:06:01,340
the instructions to build a package
is that you call that a recipe.

90
00:06:01,340 --> 00:06:04,446
And the recipe is really just a set
of instructions that are processed by

91
00:06:04,446 --> 00:06:05,288
the build engine.

92
00:06:05,288 --> 00:06:10,241
Those python, bash based scripts
that I talked about before, and

93
00:06:10,241 --> 00:06:15,028
the recipes can be defined in
files that end with .bb and .inc.

94
00:06:15,028 --> 00:06:18,833
And these are really just text files
with some combination of bash and

95
00:06:18,833 --> 00:06:19,761
python syntax.

96
00:06:21,040 --> 00:06:25,143
So the bb file contains the source and
the version information.

97
00:06:25,143 --> 00:06:28,494
So it tells you how to build
a specific version of a package,

98
00:06:28,494 --> 00:06:29,976
where to find the source.

99
00:06:29,976 --> 00:06:32,874
Maybe it's a Git hub repo
maybe it's an FTP site,

100
00:06:32,874 --> 00:06:35,988
and the version information
corresponding to that.

101
00:06:35,988 --> 00:06:39,725
And then if it exists the .inc
file contains common build and

102
00:06:39,725 --> 00:06:45,089
deploy instructions that might be similar
between different versions of the source.

103
00:06:45,089 --> 00:06:50,296
And may contain the python or
the bash fragments to actually

104
00:06:50,296 --> 00:06:55,410
perform the building and
install steps for your package.

105
00:06:55,410 --> 00:07:00,398
So the packages are going to contain
binary artifacts from the build and

106
00:07:00,398 --> 00:07:04,018
then the build outputs
are referred to as images.

107
00:07:04,018 --> 00:07:07,812
And so this is the same terminology
that build it uses as well.

108
00:07:07,812 --> 00:07:12,242
There's a binary file system
image that's like an EXT four or

109
00:07:12,242 --> 00:07:17,027
an EXT file system based image
that contains your profile system.

110
00:07:17,027 --> 00:07:21,567
There's the Linux kernel image, and
then if you have a bootloader, if you're

111
00:07:21,567 --> 00:07:26,134
running on hardware for instance,
you have a bootloader like u-boot or grub.

112
00:07:26,134 --> 00:07:28,461
That bootloader image will
also be part of the build up.

113
00:07:30,340 --> 00:07:34,452
So here's one thing that's different
about Yocto when we're talking about

114
00:07:34,452 --> 00:07:36,864
differences between the Yocto and
build it.

115
00:07:36,864 --> 00:07:43,514
The Yocto is kind of unique and that
supports what's called the layer model.

116
00:07:43,514 --> 00:07:48,050
In build it we talked about having
an external branch where we could

117
00:07:48,050 --> 00:07:52,751
define our own content that was
outside the build it project itself.

118
00:07:54,140 --> 00:07:59,416
Yocto is is a little bit more
full featured, more complex,

119
00:07:59,416 --> 00:08:05,232
it allows you to do slightly
different things than build routes.

120
00:08:05,232 --> 00:08:11,019
Be our external model where you can really
just set up arbitrary sets of directories

121
00:08:11,019 --> 00:08:16,983
that are combined together with this layer
concept into one equivalent file system.

122
00:08:16,983 --> 00:08:22,451
And so for example, if you have,
let's say we're defining our

123
00:08:22,451 --> 00:08:29,448
own meta aesd layer that has some specific
recipes and images that we define in it.

124
00:08:29,448 --> 00:08:32,088
And maybe we have a recipe in here that

125
00:08:32,088 --> 00:08:35,844
modifies something about
the way busy boxes built.

126
00:08:35,844 --> 00:08:40,721
Then there's the meta/oe recipes
that are from part of the open

127
00:08:40,721 --> 00:08:45,690
embedded project itself that define
the basics about how busy box

128
00:08:45,690 --> 00:08:49,509
normally gets built and
to find some base images.

129
00:08:49,509 --> 00:08:52,863
When we introduced the layer concept and

130
00:08:52,863 --> 00:08:56,518
we add both of these
directories as layers.

131
00:08:56,518 --> 00:09:01,302
Essentially the Yocto build system, or
bit bake build system builds a file system

132
00:09:01,302 --> 00:09:05,440
equivalent that combines all
these different layers into one.

133
00:09:05,440 --> 00:09:09,791
And the combination is based
on a hierarchy of which layer

134
00:09:09,791 --> 00:09:12,067
proceeds a different layer.

135
00:09:12,067 --> 00:09:16,989
But ultimately we combine those two into
one and make the file system equivalent of

136
00:09:16,989 --> 00:09:20,926
one directory that contains all
the different recipes, paths.

137
00:09:20,926 --> 00:09:24,765
And so for instance the meta
asd recipes core build box,

138
00:09:24,765 --> 00:09:28,531
busy box to be a pen that
customize the busy box recipe.

139
00:09:28,531 --> 00:09:31,102
That's going to show up under
recipes core busy box and

140
00:09:31,102 --> 00:09:34,381
it's going to get merged in with
the content that came from me to you.

141
00:09:34,381 --> 00:09:42,804
So we can use this layer model to
arbitrarily add to existing recipes,

142
00:09:42,804 --> 00:09:49,108
and we can also include new
recipes that are custom.

143
00:09:49,108 --> 00:09:54,065
So we can use the layers to make the build
work in a slightly different way.

144
00:09:54,065 --> 00:09:58,500
We could for instance at a patch that's
only specific to our hardware for

145
00:09:58,500 --> 00:09:59,601
a given package.

146
00:09:59,601 --> 00:10:08,191
We could configure a package to use
GUI instead of using terminal only.

147
00:10:08,191 --> 00:10:09,951
We can add support for brand new recipes.

148
00:10:09,951 --> 00:10:12,604
So in our case will do that for
aesd assignment and

149
00:10:12,604 --> 00:10:15,072
we can add support for
brand new image types.

150
00:10:15,072 --> 00:10:17,151
In our case we use that for

151
00:10:17,151 --> 00:10:21,710
core image aesd that we're
going to have a custom image.

152
00:10:21,710 --> 00:10:25,864
So in order to add a Yocto layer, you
start by doing the same thing you do for

153
00:10:25,864 --> 00:10:28,698
a build where you source poky or
the unit build in.

154
00:10:28,698 --> 00:10:31,881
And then you can use the bit
bake layers command to create

155
00:10:31,881 --> 00:10:33,583
a layer based on some source.

156
00:10:33,583 --> 00:10:36,958
In our case we have a meta
aesd directory that's going to

157
00:10:36,958 --> 00:10:39,133
contain all of our layer content.

158
00:10:39,133 --> 00:10:44,861
And then we're going to add that layer
based on the layer that we've created.

159
00:10:44,861 --> 00:10:49,261
And this is actually already done for you
in the starter repository that you use for

160
00:10:49,261 --> 00:10:50,235
assignment six.

161
00:10:50,235 --> 00:10:52,403
So this is just some
background information.

162
00:10:52,403 --> 00:10:56,457
This is all actually handled by
as a part of the build.sh script.

163
00:10:56,457 --> 00:10:59,348
So if you want to look at more
about how this is actually done,

